INT. MILL OWNER'S PARLOR - NIGHT

1903. A candle. A ledger. A decanter.

JOSEPHINE MBATHA, 30s, in a plain gray dress, stands before the desk. CYRUS ALDERWICK, 50s, the owner, writes without looking up. CONSTANCE YOON, 20s, a weaver, trembles at the door.

She sets a paper on the ledger. He reads it. He sets down the pen.

CYRUS
You'd strike over this?

JOSEPHINE
We'd strike over less.

She steps forward. She places her hand over the ledger. Constance flinches. He stands. Josephine does not.

CYRUS
Constance, did you put your name to this?

CONSTANCE
I did, sir.

JOSEPHINE ^
She did, and so did two hundred others.

He sags into his chair. He pours a glass. He drinks.

CYRUS
Tell your people we'll talk in the morning.

She nods. She steps back. She takes the girl's arm. Together they go out into the dark corridor.
